# What's included

The following enhancements are included in this PR.

## New Extensions Capabilities Model
`azd` extensions must now declare the capabilities that they support. 

Current capabilities include:
- Custom Commands (Exposes custom commands for use in azd)
- Lifecycle Events (Subscribes to lifecycle events)

## New Extensions Middleware

Handles orchestration required for extensions that leverage lifecycle events.  
- Identifies extensions that have `lifecycle-events` capatility
- Starts `azd` server, generates extension client tokens and invokes extensions.

<img width="511" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/14dc9fdd-5a62-4541-88d3-d712d691b939" />

## New EventService 

A new `EventService` exposed for extensions developers to subscribe to project/service lifecycle events

```golang
// listen.go (From microsoft.azd.demo extension)

// Create a new context that includes the AZD access token.
ctx := azdext.WithAccessToken(cmd.Context())

// Create a new AZD client.
azdClient, err := azdext.NewAzdClient()
if err != nil {
	return fmt.Errorf("failed to create azd client: %w", err)
}
defer azdClient.Close()

eventManager := azdext.NewEventManager(azdClient)
defer eventManager.Close()

// Register the event handlers
err = eventManager.AddProjectEventHandler(
	ctx,
	"preprovision",
	func(ctx context.Context, args *azdext.ProjectEventArgs) error {
		for i := 1; i <= 20; i++ {
			fmt.Printf("%d. Doing important work in extension...\n", i)
			time.Sleep(250 * time.Millisecond)
		}

		return nil
	},
)
if err != nil {
	return fmt.Errorf("failed to add preprovision project event handler: %w", err)
}

err = eventManager.AddServiceEventHandler(
	ctx,
	"prepackage",
	func(ctx context.Context, args *azdext.ServiceEventArgs) error {
		for i := 1; i <= 20; i++ {
			fmt.Printf("%d. Doing important work in extension...\n", i)
			time.Sleep(250 * time.Millisecond)
		}

		return nil
	},
	nil,
)

if err != nil {
	return fmt.Errorf("failed to add predeploy event handler: %w", err)
}

// Start listening for events
// This is a blocking call and will not return until the server connection is closed.
if err := eventManager.Receive(ctx); err != nil {
	return fmt.Errorf("failed to receive events: %w", err)
}
```

## New Authorization Model

When extensions are invoked they are provided with a JWT token.  This token must be used to authenticate with the `AzdClient`. The token includes claims to identify which extension is calling `azd` and also includes the capabilities allowed by each extension.

Failure to include this token when invoking the `AzdClient` will result in an authorization failure.

### Example decoded JWT token
```json
{
  "iss": "azd",
  "sub": "microsoft.azd.demo",
  "aud": [
    "localhost:59990"
  ],
  "exp": 1740519458,
  "iat": 1740515858,
  "cap": [
    "custom-commands",
    "lifecycle-events"
  ]
}
```

## Extension Command Updates

Minor enhancements have been made to the `install`, `upgrade` commands to allow optionally specifying an extension to install.  This is useful for testing preview extensions that may be available in alternate templates sources outside of the official `azd` extension source.

## Schema Improvements

A new `extension.schema.json` has been included for extension authors to help author `extension.yaml` files required for extensions. 

### Example YAML Extension Definition

```yaml
id: microsoft.azd.demo
namespace: demo
displayName: Demo Extension
description: This extension provides examples of the AZD extension framework.
usage: azd demo <command> [options]
version: 0.0.2
capabilities:
  - custom-commands
  - lifecycle-events
examples:
  - name: context
    description: Displays the current `azd` project & environment context.
    usage: azd demo context
  - name: prompt
    description: Display prompt capabilities.
    usage: azd demo prompt
 ```

This PR introduces localization support, building on the initial prototype from https://github.com/Automattic/wordpress-rs/pull/488.

* `wp_localization_macro::wp_messages` generates bindings from the main `ftl` file, so we can use static functions instead of string keys.
* `wp_localization_macro::WpDeriveLocalizable` derive macro implements the `Display` trait if the type implements `wp_localization::WpSupportsLocalization` trait. It also generates `uniffi` bindings to localize the type using a free-floating function with the name `localize_{type_name}`.
* Implementing `wp_localization::WpSupportsLocalization` is how we'll bind a type to a localized message using the bindings generated in `wp_localization::WpMessages`.
* `wp_localization` depends on `fluent`, but it provides a tool agnostic API to `wp_api`, so we can replace `fluent` without changing anything about how we localize our types in `wp_api`.

Here is an example usage:

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, thiserror::Error, uniffi::Error, WpDeriveLocalizable)]
pub enum ParseApiRootUrlError {
    ApiRootLinkHeaderNotFound {
        status_code: u16,
        header_map: Arc<WpNetworkHeaderMap>,
    },
}

impl WpSupportsLocalization for ParseApiRootUrlError {
    fn message_bundle(&self) -> wp_localization::MessageBundle {
        match self {
            ParseApiRootUrlError::ApiRootLinkHeaderNotFound {
                status_code,
                header_map,
            } => WpMessages::api_root_link_header_not_found(
                status_code.to_string(),
                format!("{:#?}", header_map),
            ),
        }
    }
}
```

Notice how we are no longer using the `#[error("..")` attribute from `thiserror` which would implement the `Display` trait. Instead, we only implement `WpSupportsLocalization` and add the `WpDeriveLocalizable`.

Once this is implemented, here are few usage options:
```rust
// Rust
let error = ParseApiRootUrlError::ApiRootLinkHeaderNotFound {
    status_code: 404,
    header_map: some_header_map(),
};
let string_with_default_locale = error.to_string();
let string_with_default_locale = error.localize(None);
let string_with_specified_locale = error.localize(Some(wp_localization::WpLocale::TrTR));
```
```kotlin
// Kotlin
val error = ParseApiRootUrlException.ApiRootLinkHeaderNotFound(
    statusCode = 404u,
    headerMap = WpNetworkHeaderMap.fromMap(mapOf())
)
val stringWithDefaultLocale = localizeParseApiRootUrlError(error, locale = null)
val stringWithSpecifiedLocale = localizeParseApiRootUrlError(error, locale = WpLocale.TR_TR)
```

I believe this is a great starting point for localization. As far as I can tell, it should be safe to use this in production. Having said that, there is still quite a bit room for improvement. There are `fluent` types we don't handle at all, we could use more tests, the bindings don't seem to be generated after `ftl` file is updated until `cargo build`, (although there is a build script that tries to do that) and uniffi bindings are free-floating functions. (even though initially they were contained in a `UniffiLocalizable` type) If I don't call this done here I feel like I might spend another few days on it and probably won't have enough to show for it. To keep things efficient, I'd like to re-visit these topics and any other improvements on a need-to basis.
